---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import TipCalculator from '@components/islands/TipCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'tip-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate a tip?', answer: 'Multiply the bill by the tip percentage as a decimal. For a $50 bill at 20%, that is 50 × 0.20 = $10, for a $60 total. A quick mental shortcut for 20% is to take 10% (move the decimal one place: $5) and double it.' },
  { question: 'How much should I tip?', answer: 'In the United States, 15–20% of the pre-tax bill is standard for sit-down restaurant service, with 18–20% for good service. Tip 10% or less for minimal service, and more for exceptional service. Norms differ by country and service type.' },
  { question: 'How much should I tip movers, a hairdresser or an Uber driver?', answer: 'Common US guidance: movers about $20–40 per person for a day\'s work (or 5–10% of the bill split among the crew), hairdressers and barbers 15–20%, massage therapists 15–20%, and rideshare or taxi drivers 10–20% with a small minimum. See the table below for more.' },
  { question: 'Do I tip on the total with tax, or before tax?', answer: 'Etiquette says to tip on the pre-tax amount, though many people tip on the total for simplicity. Enter whichever figure you prefer as the bill.' },
  { question: 'How do I split a bill with a tip?', answer: 'Enter the number of people and the calculator divides the total (bill plus tip) evenly, showing the amount and the tip each person owes. A $60 total split four ways is $15 each.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" intro="Calculate the tip and total on any bill and split it evenly between friends. Tap a preset percentage or enter your own." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-23' }}>
  <TipCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the <strong>bill</strong> amount.</li>
      <li>Set a <strong>tip percentage</strong> — tap a preset or type your own.</li>
      <li>Enter the <strong>number of people</strong> sharing the bill (a whole number, at least one).</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see the total per person, plus the tip, the grand total and the tip each person owes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How to calculate a tip</h2>
    <p>
      Multiply the bill by the tip rate: a <strong>$50</strong> bill at <strong>20%</strong> is
      50 × 0.20 = <strong>$10</strong>, for a $60 total. The fastest mental trick for 20% is to
      find 10% — just move the decimal one place, to $5 — and double it; for 15%, take that 10%
      and add half of it again. The calculator does the exact arithmetic and the split in one step.
    </p>

    <h2>The math behind the split</h2>
    <p>From the bill and the tip rate, the tool derives four figures:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tip amount</strong> = bill × (tip % ÷ 100)</li>
      <li><strong>Total</strong> = bill + tip</li>
      <li><strong>Tip per person</strong> = tip ÷ number of people</li>
      <li><strong>Total per person</strong> = total ÷ number of people</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>$50</strong> bill at <strong>20%</strong> adds a <strong>$10</strong> tip for a
      <strong>$60</strong> total. Split four ways, that is <strong>$15 each</strong>, of which
      <strong>$2.50</strong> is the tip. Change any input and the rest update together — raise the
      tip to 25% and the total climbs to $62.50, or $15.63 a head.
    </p>

    <h2>How much to tip, by service</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Service</th><th>Typical US tip</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Restaurant (table service)</td><td>15–20% pre-tax</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Food delivery</td><td>15–20% (min. $3–5)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rideshare / taxi</td><td>10–20%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hairdresser / barber</td><td>15–20%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Massage therapist</td><td>15–20%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Movers</td><td>$20–40 per mover / day</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bartender</td><td>$1–2 per drink</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hotel housekeeping</td><td>$2–5 per night</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Pre-tax or post-tax?</h2>
    <p>
      Etiquette says to tip on the <strong>pre-tax</strong> bill, since the tax is not part of the
      service — though many people tip on the total for simplicity, and on a typical bill the
      difference is small. Enter whichever figure you prefer as the bill amount; the calculator
      tips on the number you give it.
    </p>

    <h2>Tipping around the world</h2>
    <p>
      Customs vary widely by country — in much of Europe and Asia a service charge is often already
      included and tipping is minimal or rounded up rather than a set percentage. When travelling,
      check local norms so you neither under- nor over-tip. One limitation to note: this tool splits
      the bill <em>evenly</em> across whole people, so it will not divide an uneven share or split by
      individual orders.
    </p>
    <p>
      To add or remove sales tax from a price, see the
      <a href="/finance/sales-tax-calculator">sales tax calculator</a>. This is general information
      to help you tip and split a bill, not etiquette or financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
